          "focus": "Describe the overall organization and structural progression of the text",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 9 · Q21",
          "passageTitle": "Overall Structure — Q21",
          "passage": "In Ojibwe, an Indigenous language from the\nGreat Lakes region of what are now the United\nStates and Canada, wása means \"far,\" whereas\nwa-wasa means \"far apart.\" This phenomenon,\nin which an element of a root word is repeated,\nsometimes with modification, within another\nword that is related to the root word, is called\nreduplication. In this case, the element \"wa\" in\nwása gets repeated in wa-wasa. There are many\nexamples of this type of reduplication in Ojibwe.",
