          "passage": "The following text is adapted from George\nEliot's 1857 short story \"The Sad Fortunes of\nthe Rev. Amos Barton. \" Mr. Ely is a\nclergyman in the town of Milby.\nBy the laity of Milby and its neighbourhood\n[Mr. Ely] was regarded as a man of quite\nremarkable powers and learning, who must make\na considerable sensation in London pulpits and\ndrawing-rooms on his occasional visit to the\nmetropolis; and by his brother clergy he was\nregarded as a discreet and agreeable fellow. Mr.\nEly never got into a warm discussion; he\nsuggested what might be thought, but rarely\nsaid what he thought himself; he never let either\nmen or women see that he was laughing at\nthem, and he never gave any one an opportunity\nof laughing at him.",
