          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 9 · Q27",
          "passageTitle": "Overall Structure — Q27",
          "passage": "Spoken in Northern California, Karuk is an\nisolate, or a language that has no demonstrated\nrelationship to other documented languages.\nThe Haida language in Alaska is an isolate as\nwell. However, most Native languages belong to\nlanguage families, or groups of languages whose\nsimilarities can be attributed to shared descent\nfrom a single language spoken thousands of\nyears ago. Curiously, isolates are more prevalent\nin regions where numerous nonisolates are also\nspoken. Thus, the geographical circumstances\nthat favor the development of isolates may also\ndrive diversification within language families.",
          "prompt": "Which choice best describes the overall structure\nof the text?",
